WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.909
Film Courage: How many pitch meetings will
an executive usually have in one day?

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:05.640
Because [as a screenwriter looking to pitch]
we’re going in, we’re ten minutes early,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.049
we’ve got our best outfit on, business casual
(I don’t know what we’re wearing?) but

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:15.610
something that makes us feel comfortable,
we’re ready, this is the biggest deal to

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.779
us, but this person [we are pitching to] has
already seen how many people beforehand?

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:25.910
Carole Kirschner: When I was at CBS in comedy
development we would meet with people at 10:00

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:36.079
a.m., 11:00 a.m., 12:00 p.m., 3:00 p.m., 4:00
p.m. and 5:00 p.m.

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:39.250
So here’s a tip: get the early morning meeting.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.090
Do not get a late afternoon meeting.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.859
Especially don’t get a late afternoon meeting
on Fridays.

00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:49.649
If you can get a morning meeting, that is
the best way to do it.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:55.390
Executives are experienced at taking meetings,
they know how to do that.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:01.640
So if you have to get an afternoon meeting,
it’s not going to sink you.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.390
But your pitch has to be great.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:10.280
If your pitch is great then they will come
alive because they are looking for great pitches

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.810
because it makes their job easier.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.130
So if you have an engaging pitch, they will
come alive.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.439
If the pitch is boring in the late afternoon,
it’s harder to pay attention to it.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:25.570
Film Courage: Let’s talk about working on
your pitch.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.500
Because, again we see ourselves one way, others
see us another way.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.259
Are we coming up for a script for our pitch?

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.909
Or is it better to be spontaneous?

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:37.890
Carole Kirschner: Don’t be spontaneous!

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.890
It should be rehearsed.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.009
It’s a performance.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:48.310
It shouldn’t be memorized from within an
inch of its life, that is not a good idea.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.790
You should know it backwards and forwards
and sideways.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:59.360
Then put away the paper (although you can
have notes if you want) and then make it a

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.360
conversation.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.710
Do not leave it to “I’m just going to
walk in and do this.”

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.790
No.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.259
It’s like people in school.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.310
You want to be an overachiever.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.630
Be completely prepared.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.190
Not memorized but completely prepared.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:20.940
I have a former mentee who sold a lot of pilots
scripts.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.390
She never goes in [to pitch] unless she’s
practiced eight times.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.930
She practices in front of people.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.520
She practices in front of a mirror.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.440
She practices and practices.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.890
There was a writer/producer who was phenomenal
named Stephen Cannell.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:45.540
He was the most prolific television producer
and creator in the business.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.140
He was always practicing his pitch.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.750
I knew his daughter and she said “You know,
we couldn’t go to the store without him

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.110
pitching.”

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:02.811
If he was walking down the hallway in his
building, he would buttonhole you and tell

00:03:02.811 --> 00:03:05.020
you the pitch and ask for feedback.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.440
Practice, practice, practice.

